  "notes": "A 1970s-lineage soul singer relaunched inside the neo-soul frame, ceded to this section by `rnb-2000s`. The band is the entry's 68–98 straight on 2&4, sitting in the slow half of the lane. The harmonic device the research actually names is the load-bearing one: \"a sampled/interpolated 70s-soul chord bed re-voiced over live keys, giving 7ths and 9ths on every chord\" — so the triad weight drops to 0.04 and sevenths and ninths carry the whole spread, and the two families are the ones the entry writes out. Drums are \"live-feel kit with the snare back and heavy, no 16th hat carpet, tambourine on the backbeat, and a looped-breakbeat option — the records sit between a hip-hop loop and a played kit\", which is the wide offGrid window against a modest ghost probability, an 8th hat base at a low fill density and a tambourine-led percussion set. Melody is contralto and 70s-phrased rather than Badu-lagged: \"big-toned, long sustained notes into melismatic tags\", hence the low register, the legato articulation, the long arc and `endVariation` for the tags. Bass is \"thick, root-and-octave with blues fills\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum numbers, all melody and bass numbers, and the scale weighting — the research names no specific keys, so none are authored and the model inherits the lane's. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it medium and says the drum and tempo parameters are lane inference, which it marks [LOW-CONF].",
